{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 211 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a11554f058166e3005470a94d047bd310f0013f1e9c817d3da25682138bf4185" :edb/db-before-t 210 :edb/db-after-t 211 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533523 50 #inst "2026-10-11T05:35:30.447Z" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186188279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406872 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186188279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406874 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186188287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406871 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186188287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406873 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186188931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187632 13194139533523 17592186045585 false] [17592186188931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406867 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186188931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186188931 1071 "Let $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $Im(T) = {[x, y, z]^{T}: 3x + 2y - z = 0}$ then $nullity(T) = 1$\n2. If $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx = [1,-2, 1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible\n3. If $v_{1}$, $v_{2}$, and $v_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and $v_{3} = v_{1} + 2v_{2}$, then $Ker(T) = {0}$" 13194139533523 17592186045585 false] [17592186188931 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,3x+2y-z=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n- If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible\n- If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186188931 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406840 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406875 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406840 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406876 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406840 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406877 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406840 1043 