e] [17592186406870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406845 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406862 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406864 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406865 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406870 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406870 1077 "selection" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406870 1079 #edb/ref 17592186406865 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406870 1080 #uuid "b752c3d9-0b1a-5d80-8a37-82a98660d651" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406868 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406871 1069 "q-140098" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406871 1071 "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y-2z=0}.$" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406871 1072 #uuid "7bb2599b-79c3-5637-9b4b-ca26bce210be" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406871 1074 "Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406870 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406872 1069 "q-84081" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406872 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n- If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$, then $T$ is invertible" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406872 1072 #uuid "838322c6-867e-5a73-a18d-bbf81bd1218b" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406872 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $Tx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406869 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406873 1069 "q-83446" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406873 1071 "Find $\\text{rank}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x+y+z=0}.$" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406873 1072 #uuid "94b1ad7b-577b-5c7c-8164-0f25bc8b5263" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406873 1074 "Solving $x+y+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=-x-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ -x-y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406866 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406874 1069 "q-140100" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406874 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ * \n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$\n- $T$ is a bijection" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406874 1072 #uuid "b72abcb6-85da-5c4d-a8eb-72ce20ae6b72" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406874 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\"" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406875 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406876 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406875 1062 #uuid "4b0b57c7-2d59-5d20-9983-005e3257de52" 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406875 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406872 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406876 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406877 13194139533523 17592186045585 true] [17592186406876 1062 #uuid "81a2fa92-8011-53d7-b83d-7c048c758080" 13194139533